{"availability":"available","full":"alejandro-publius/second-look","name":"second-look","description":"A two-minute test that measures how well a volunteer sees creek damage, carried as FHIR beside every observation. OneAquaHealth Hackathon 2026.","category":"Other","language":"Python","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-20T21:00:43Z","updated":"2026-10-04T08:14:57Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://second-look-79t.pages.dev","topics":["ai-evaluation","citizen-science","cloudflare-workers","environmental-health","fhir","hackathon","hl7-fhir","nextjs","oneaquahealth","pwa","rivers","water-quality"],"demo":"https://second-look-79t.pages.dev/t2/demo","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.387Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.387Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.387Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.387Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Next.js","Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Track 3, AI-Supported Assessment. The track says citizen observations can be inconsistent and error-prone. We measure that with a two-minute photo test. Each volunteer gets a score for each of four kinds of creek damage, saved with every observation they make. The AI takes the same test. It may only ask a volunteer to look again, only where it passed, and only after the volunteer has answered. The AI's help must be measured, not assumed. A second test, planned before anyone took it, is designed to check whether that one question makes people more accurate; its benefit is not yet established.…","People judge a creek the way they judge a park. Tidy and green reads as healthy. OneAquaHealth's project lead said it in the first workshop: volunteers catch smell, foam and colour, and walk past concrete banks, a channel that was dug out, and pretty plants that do not belong. So the best-looking creek can get the best rating and deserve the worst.","Professional surveyors fixed this long ago. In the UK's River Habitat Survey, \"only surveys from accredited surveyors will be entered on the RHS database\", and accreditation means a course and a test (RHS manual 2003, pages 3 and 20; docs/notes/sources.md). Some volunteer programs certify people for a method, such as water chemistry. None we know of measures how well each volunteer sees each feature, or keeps that score with every observation, so a volunteer's observation arrives with no mark of how well its observer sees."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/alejandro-publius/second-look/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"8652e85f21623f48d3e367b037b484aa1a90858c9c12a90d13d1eea1cd673e47","extractedAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.607Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.607Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Track 3, AI-Supported Assessment. The track says citizen observations can be inconsistent and error-prone. We measure that with a two-minute photo test. Each volunteer gets a score for each of four kinds of creek damage, saved with every observation they make. The AI takes the same test. It may only ask a volunteer to look again, only where it passed, and only after the volunteer has answered. The AI's help must be measured, not assumed. A second test, planned before anyone took it, is designed to check whether that one question makes people more accurate; its benefit is not yet established.…","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/alejandro-publius/second-look/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"8652e85f21623f48d3e367b037b484aa1a90858c9c12a90d13d1eea1cd673e47","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/alejandro-publius/second-look/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.608Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-10-04T08:14:57Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/alejandro-publius/second-look/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-04T08:16:29.611Z","hash":"8652e85f21623f48d3e367b037b484aa1a90858c9c12a90d1